---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/licenses.json';

interface Licence { name: string; url: string }
interface Component {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  version: string;
  npm: string | null;
  licences: Licence[];
  project: string;
  source: string;
  shipped: string;
  kind: 'library' | 'wasm' | 'model' | 'font';
  via?: string;
  copyleft?: 'lgpl' | 'mpl';
  copyright?: string;
  replace?: string;
  attribution?: string;
  note?: string;
}
const all = data.components as Component[];

const copyleft = all.filter((c) => c.copyleft);
const lgpl = copyleft.filter((c) => c.copyleft === 'lgpl');
const mpl = copyleft.filter((c) => c.copyleft === 'mpl');
const attributions = all.filter((c) => c.attribution);
const childrenOf = (id: string) => all.filter((c) => c.via === id);
const libraries = all.filter((c) => (c.kind === 'library' || c.kind === 'wasm') && !c.via);
const models = all.filter((c) => c.kind === 'model');
const fonts = all.filter((c) => c.kind === 'font' && !c.via);
---
<Base title="Open-source licenses" description="Every library, WebAssembly module, AI model and font Stayput ships, with its version, license and a link to the exact source. Includes the source offer for the LGPL and MPL parts.">
  <article class="wrap-narrow prose page licenses">
    <h1>Open-source licenses</h1>
    <p>Stayput is MIT licensed and stands on a lot of other people's open-source work. This page lists every library, WebAssembly module, AI model and font the site ships to your browser, with its version, its license and a link to the exact source. It is generated from one data file in <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/src/data/licenses.json" rel="noopener">the repository</a>, and a test fails when a dependency is missing from it.</p>

    <h2 id="source-offer">Source code, and your right to replace or relink the LGPL parts</h2>
    <p>A few components are under the GNU Lesser General Public License (LGPL) or the Mozilla Public License 2.0 (MPL). Both let anyone use them in a project like this one. They also give you rights over those components, so here is how to use them.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Source.</strong> Each LGPL and MPL component below links to the exact version that Stayput ships: a release tag or a commit in its upstream repository. The source for Stayput's own code, including the build script that copies the decoders into place, is in the <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput" rel="noopener">repository</a>. If a link ever stops working, tell us at <a href="mailto:hello@stayput.dev">hello@stayput.dev</a> and we will fix it or send you the source.</li>
      <li><strong>They are separate files.</strong> The LGPL parts are not merged into Stayput's own JavaScript. HEIC support is one file (<code>/vendor/heic-to@1.6.6/heic-to.min.js</code>, which holds libheif and libde265), MP3 encoding is another (<code>/vendor/wasm-media-encoders@0.7.0/mp3.wasm</code>, which is LAME), opening password-protected 7z and RAR archives is a third (<code>/vendor/7z-wasm@1.2.0/7zz.wasm</code>, which is 7-Zip), and opening AVI files is a fourth set (<code>/vendor/libav.js@6.10.9.0/libav-6.10.9.0-avi.wasm.wasm</code> with two small JavaScript files beside it, which is FFmpeg's libraries; see <a href="#ffmpeg">below</a>, reading FLV files is a fifth (<code>/vendor/libav.js@6.10.9.0/libav-6.10.9.0-flv.wasm.wasm</code>, built the same way from the same libraries), reading 3GP phone videos a sixth (<code>/vendor/libav.js@6.10.9.0/libav-6.10.9.0-3gp.wasm.wasm</code>, likewise), and WMA files a seventh (<code>/vendor/libav.js@6.10.9.0/libav-6.10.9.0-wma.wasm.wasm</code>; see <a href="#ffmpeg-wma">below</a>). The page loads each by URL when a tool needs it and does not check what is inside, so a modified or newer build of the same library works as a drop-in.</li>
      <li><strong>Replace or relink them on your own copy.</strong> Clone the repository, point the entry for the library in <code>src/data/vendor.json</code> at your build (or install your build in place of the npm package), run <code>npm run build</code> and serve the <code>dist</code> folder. The build copies the file into <code>/vendor/</code> and nothing else needs to change.</li>
      <li><strong>Replace them in your browser, without building anything.</strong> Use your browser's local overrides, or an extension that rewrites requests, to answer the URL above with your own file. Turn on "Bypass for network" in the developer tools for the visit, because the site's service worker caches these files. You may also reverse engineer the LGPL parts as far as needed to debug your changes.</li>
      <li><strong>MPL parts.</strong> Mediabunny and Eigen are under MPL-2.0, which applies to their own files only. Their source is linked below. Stayput's own files stay MIT, and any change you make to a file under the MPL has to be published under the MPL.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Full license texts: <a href="/licenses/LGPL-3.0.txt">GNU LGPL 3.0</a> (it builds on the <a href="https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.txt" rel="noopener">GNU GPL 3.0</a>), <a href="/licenses/LGPL-2.0.txt">GNU Library GPL 2.0</a>, <a href="/licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt">GNU Lesser General Public License 2.1</a> and <a href="/licenses/MPL-2.0.txt">Mozilla Public License 2.0</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="ffmpeg">FFmpeg's libraries, for AVI and FLV files</h2>
    <p>Most video runs on your browser's WebCodecs through Mediabunny. For old formats the browser cannot read, the page loads a decoder built from FFmpeg's libraries (LGPL) only when you convert such a file.</p>
    <p>Today that is AVI and FLV. The AVI decoder is FFmpeg 9.0's libavformat, libavcodec, libswscale and libswresample, compiled to WebAssembly with <a href="https://github.com/Yahweasel/libav.js" rel="noopener">libav.js</a> 6.10.9.0. Both are licensed <a href="/licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt">LGPL-2.1-or-later</a>. The build contains the AVI demuxer and decoders for MPEG-4 Part 2 video (Xvid, DivX 4 and later), Motion JPEG, MP3, MP2, AC-3 and PCM sound, and nothing else: it was configured with <code>--disable-everything</code>, with no GPL or non-free parts. This software is based in part on the work of the Independent JPEG Group.</p>
    <p>The FLV reader is a second, smaller build of the same FFmpeg 9.0 libraries (libavformat and libavcodec only) and the same libav.js 6.10.9.0, also <a href="/licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt">LGPL-2.1-or-later</a>. It contains the FLV demuxer and the MP3 decoder and nothing else, configured with <code>--disable-everything</code>: no H.264 or AAC decoder, and none for VP6, Sorenson Spark, Windows Media or VC-1. An FLV's H.264 picture and AAC sound are copied into the MP4 as they are, not decoded. Its config is <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/vendor-builds/libav-flv/config.json" rel="noopener"><code>vendor-builds/libav-flv/config.json</code></a> and its build script and README are in <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/tree/main/vendor-builds/libav-flv" rel="noopener"><code>vendor-builds/libav-flv/</code></a>. It is three separate files like the AVI one, <code>libav-6.10.9.0-flv.mjs</code>, <code>libav-6.10.9.0-flv.wasm.mjs</code> and <code>libav-6.10.9.0-flv.wasm.wasm</code> in <code>/vendor/libav.js@6.10.9.0/</code>, fetched only when you convert an FLV.</p>
    <p>The 3GP decoder is a third build of the same FFmpeg 9.0 libraries and the same libav.js 6.10.9.0, also <a href="/licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt">LGPL-2.1-or-later</a>. It contains the MOV demuxer (which reads 3GP, 3G2 and MP4) and the decoders for MPEG-4 Part 2 and H.263 video and for AMR-NB and AMR-WB sound, which are FFmpeg's own code, and nothing else, configured with <code>--disable-everything</code>: no H.264 or AAC decoder (those are copied into the MP4 as they are), and none for Windows Media, VC-1 or VP6. Its config is <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/vendor-builds/libav-3gp/config.json" rel="noopener"><code>vendor-builds/libav-3gp/config.json</code></a> and its build script and README are in <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/tree/main/vendor-builds/libav-3gp" rel="noopener"><code>vendor-builds/libav-3gp/</code></a>. It is three separate files, <code>libav-6.10.9.0-3gp.mjs</code>, <code>libav-6.10.9.0-3gp.wasm.mjs</code> and <code>libav-6.10.9.0-3gp.wasm.wasm</code> in <code>/vendor/libav.js@6.10.9.0/</code>, fetched only when you convert a 3GP.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Exact source.</strong> The FFmpeg 9.0 release tarball is <a href="https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.tar.xz" rel="noopener">ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.tar.xz</a> (git tag <a href="https://github.com/FFmpeg/FFmpeg/tree/refs/tags/n9.0" rel="noopener">n9.0</a>). The libav.js source is the git tag <a href="https://github.com/Yahweasel/libav.js/tree/v6.10.9.0" rel="noopener">v6.10.9.0</a>. The <a href="https://registry.npmjs.org/libav.js/-/libav.js-6.10.9.tgz" rel="noopener">libav.js 6.10.9 npm package</a> carries both source tarballs in its <code>sources</code> folder, and the build below uses nothing else.</li>
      <li><strong>How it was built.</strong> libav.js's <code>configs/mkconfig.js</code> turned the list in <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/vendor-builds/libav-avi/config.json" rel="noopener"><code>vendor-builds/libav-avi/config.json</code></a> into FFmpeg configure flags (<a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/vendor-builds/libav-avi/dist/ffmpeg-config.txt" rel="noopener">the result</a>), and <code>make</code> built it inside the <code>emscripten/emsdk:6.0.5</code> Docker image. <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/vendor-builds/libav-avi/build.sh" rel="noopener"><code>vendor-builds/libav-avi/build.sh</code></a> does all of it from a clean machine; the <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/vendor-builds/libav-avi/README.md" rel="noopener">README beside it</a> explains the steps and lists which codecs are in and out, with the patent-expiry evidence for each.</li>
      <li><strong>Replaceable.</strong> The decoder is three separate files, <code>/vendor/libav.js@6.10.9.0/libav-6.10.9.0-avi.mjs</code>, <code>libav-6.10.9.0-avi.wasm.mjs</code> and <code>libav-6.10.9.0-avi.wasm.wasm</code>, fetched from this site by URL only when you convert an AVI. They are not merged into Stayput's own JavaScript, so a modified build of the same three files works as a drop-in (see the instructions above).</li>
      <li><strong>Your file.</strong> It is read in slices from your disk by the decoder running in a background worker in your tab. Nothing about the file is sent anywhere.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="ffmpeg-wma">FFmpeg's libraries, for WMA files</h2>
    <p>Most video runs on your browser's WebCodecs through Mediabunny. For old formats the browser cannot read, the page loads a decoder built from FFmpeg's libraries (LGPL) only when you convert such a file.</p>
    <p>For WMA that decoder is a second, smaller build of FFmpeg 9.0's libavformat, libavcodec and libswresample, compiled to WebAssembly with <a href="https://github.com/Yahweasel/libav.js" rel="noopener">libav.js</a> 6.10.9.0, both licensed <a href="/licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt">LGPL-2.1-or-later</a>. It contains the ASF demuxer and the decoders for Windows Media Audio versions 1 and 2, and nothing else: it was configured with <code>--disable-everything</code>, with no GPL or non-free parts, and without WMA Pro, WMA Lossless, WMA Voice, Windows Media Video, VC-1 or VP6.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Exact source.</strong> The same as for AVI above: the FFmpeg 9.0 tarball (git tag <a href="https://github.com/FFmpeg/FFmpeg/tree/refs/tags/n9.0" rel="noopener">n9.0</a>) and libav.js <a href="https://github.com/Yahweasel/libav.js/tree/v6.10.9.0" rel="noopener">v6.10.9.0</a>, both inside the <a href="https://registry.npmjs.org/libav.js/-/libav.js-6.10.9.tgz" rel="noopener">libav.js 6.10.9 npm package</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>How it was built.</strong> The list in <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/vendor-builds/libav-wma/config.json" rel="noopener"><code>vendor-builds/libav-wma/config.json</code></a> became FFmpeg configure flags (<a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/vendor-builds/libav-wma/dist/ffmpeg-config.txt" rel="noopener">the result</a>), and <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/vendor-builds/libav-wma/build.sh" rel="noopener"><code>vendor-builds/libav-wma/build.sh</code></a> builds it from a clean machine, with the <a href="https://github.com/keenanlk/stayput/blob/main/vendor-builds/libav-wma/README.md" rel="noopener">README beside it</a> listing what is in and out.</li>
      <li><strong>Replaceable.</strong> The decoder is three separate files, <code>/vendor/libav.js@6.10.9.0/libav-6.10.9.0-wma.mjs</code>, <code>libav-6.10.9.0-wma.wasm.mjs</code> and <code>libav-6.10.9.0-wma.wasm.wasm</code>, fetched from this site by URL only when you convert a WMA, so a modified build of the same three files works as a drop-in.</li>
      <li><strong>Your file.</strong> It is read in slices from your disk by the decoder running in a background worker in your tab. Nothing about the file is sent anywhere.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="lgpl">Components under the LGPL</h2>
    <ul class="lic-list lic-cards">
      {lgpl.map((c) => (
        <li class="lic-card" id={c.id}>
          <b>{c.name} <span class="lic-ver">{c.version}</span></b>
          <span>License: {c.licences.map((l, i) => <><a href={l.url} rel="noopener">{l.name}</a>{i < c.licences.length - 1 ? ', ' : ''}</>)}</span>
          {c.copyright && <span>Copyright: {c.copyright}</span>}
          <span>Source for this exact version: <a href={c.source} rel="noopener">{c.source.replace('https://', '')}</a></span>
          <span>Shipped as: {c.shipped}</span>
          {c.replace && <span>To replace it, serve your own file at <code>{c.replace}</code>.</span>}
          {c.note && <span class="muted">{c.note}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="mpl">Components under the MPL-2.0</h2>
    <ul class="lic-list lic-cards">
      {mpl.map((c) => (
        <li class="lic-card" id={c.id}>
          <b>{c.name} <span class="lic-ver">{c.version}</span></b>
          <span>License: {c.licences.map((l, i) => <><a href={l.url} rel="noopener">{l.name}</a>{i < c.licences.length - 1 ? ', ' : ''}</>)}</span>
          {c.copyright && <span>Copyright: {c.copyright}</span>}
          <span>Source for this exact version: <a href={c.source} rel="noopener">{c.source.replace('https://', '')}</a></span>
          <span>Shipped as: {c.shipped}</span>
          {c.replace && <span>To replace it, serve your own file at <code>{c.replace}</code>.</span>}
          {c.note && <span class="muted">{c.note}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    {attributions.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2 id="notices">Notices</h2>
        <ul class="lic-list lic-cards">
          {attributions.map((c) => (
            <li class="lic-card">
              <b>{c.name} <span class="lic-ver">{c.version}</span></b>
              <span>{c.attribution}</span>
              <span>Copyright: {c.copyright}</span>
              <span>License text: {c.licences.map((l, i) => <><a href={l.url} rel="noopener">{l.name}</a>{i < c.licences.length - 1 ? ', ' : ''}</>)}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </>
    )}

    <h2 id="libraries">Libraries and WebAssembly modules</h2>
    <p>Each item names the library, then what is compiled or bundled inside it. Versions are the ones in the current build.</p>
    <ul class="lic-list">
      {libraries.map((c) => (
        <li id={`i-${c.id}`}>
          <b>{c.name} <span class="lic-ver">{c.version}</span></b>
          <span class="lic-meta">
            {c.licences.map((l, i) => <><a href={l.url} rel="noopener">{l.name}</a>{i < c.licences.length - 1 ? ', ' : ''}</>)}
            {' · '}<a href={c.project} rel="noopener">Project</a>
            {' · '}<a href={c.source} rel="noopener">Source</a>
          </span>
          <span class="muted">{c.shipped}.{c.note ? ` ${c.note}` : ''}</span>
          {childrenOf(c.id).length > 0 && (
            <ul class="lic-sub">
              {childrenOf(c.id).map((k) => (
                <li>
                  <b>{k.name} <span class="lic-ver">{k.version}</span></b>
                  <span class="lic-meta">
                    {k.licences.map((l, i) => <><a href={l.url} rel="noopener">{l.name}</a>{i < k.licences.length - 1 ? ', ' : ''}</>)}
                    {' · '}<a href={k.project} rel="noopener">Project</a>
                    {' · '}<a href={k.source} rel="noopener">Source</a>
                  </span>
                  {k.note && <span class="muted">{k.note}</span>}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          )}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="models">AI models</h2>
    <p>The models are files on this site, loaded by the tool that needs them and run on your device. Each license below allows redistribution; a note flags anything unusual about a model's training data.</p>
    <ul class="lic-list">
      {models.map((c) => (
        <li id={`i-${c.id}`}>
          <b>{c.name} <span class="lic-ver">{c.version}</span></b>
          <span class="lic-meta">
            {c.licences.map((l, i) => <><a href={l.url} rel="noopener">{l.name}</a>{i < c.licences.length - 1 ? ', ' : ''}</>)}
            {' · '}<a href={c.project} rel="noopener">Project</a>
            {' · '}<a href={c.source} rel="noopener">Source</a>
          </span>
          <span class="muted">{c.shipped}{c.note ? `. ${c.note}` : ''}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="fonts">Fonts and other files</h2>
    <ul class="lic-list">
      {fonts.map((c) => (
        <li id={`i-${c.id}`}>
          <b>{c.name} <span class="lic-ver">{c.version}</span></b>
          <span class="lic-meta">
            {c.licences.map((l, i) => <><a href={l.url} rel="noopener">{l.name}</a>{i < c.licences.length - 1 ? ', ' : ''}</>)}
            {' · '}<a href={c.project} rel="noopener">Project</a>
            {' · '}<a href={c.source} rel="noopener">Source</a>
          </span>
          <span class="muted">{c.shipped}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>Back to <a href="/about">About Stayput</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
